1
00:00:00,160 --> 00:00:02,800
Wi Fi is perfect next to the router and

2
00:00:02,800 --> 00:00:04,240
almost dead in one room.

3
00:00:04,320 --> 00:00:07,040
In ideal open space, move twice as far and

4
00:00:07,040 --> 00:00:09,200
you receive a quarter of the power.

5
00:00:09,200 --> 00:00:10,960
But that's only half the story.

6
00:00:10,960 --> 00:00:13,040
The rest is in your walls.

7
00:00:13,040 --> 00:00:14,880
Let's follow your signal from the router

8
00:00:14,880 --> 00:00:17,440
to that room, with one law of physics and

9
00:00:17,440 --> 00:00:19,040
a little algebra.

10
00:00:19,040 --> 00:00:21,360
Think of your router as a tiny light bulb

11
00:00:21,360 --> 00:00:22,560
for radio waves.

12
00:00:22,560 --> 00:00:25,040
In ideal open space, its power spreads

13
00:00:25,040 --> 00:00:27,280
outward. One meter away, it covers a

14
00:00:27,280 --> 00:00:29,120
sphere one meter in radius.

15
00:00:29,120 --> 00:00:31,440
Two meters away, the same power covers a

16
00:00:31,440 --> 00:00:32,960
sphere twice as wide.

17
00:00:32,960 --> 00:00:36,080
A sphere's area is four pi r squared.

18
00:00:36,080 --> 00:00:38,320
Double r, and the area grows by two

19
00:00:38,480 --> 00:00:40,160
squared, four times.

20
00:00:40,160 --> 00:00:42,160
So every square meter gets a quarter as

21
00:00:42,160 --> 00:00:42,630
much.

22
00:00:42,640 --> 00:00:44,960
As an equation, the power passing through

23
00:00:44,960 --> 00:00:47,360
each square meter, at distance r, is the

24
00:00:47,360 --> 00:00:50,960
power P divided by four pi r squared.

25
00:00:50,960 --> 00:00:52,960
That's the inverse square law.

26
00:00:52,960 --> 00:00:55,120
Your phone's antenna catches a share of

27
00:00:55,120 --> 00:00:57,200
that, so if the antennas and frequency

28
00:00:57,200 --> 00:00:58,630
stay the same, the power your phone

29
00:00:58,640 --> 00:01:00,880
receives follows the same rule.

30
00:01:00,880 --> 00:01:02,720
At twice the distance, you're left with

31
00:01:02,720 --> 00:01:03,840
one over two squared.

32
00:01:04,190 --> 00:01:06,670
One quarter. Three times as far, one

33
00:01:06,830 --> 00:01:10,110
ninth. Ten times as far, one hundredth.

34
00:01:10,110 --> 00:01:11,550
Here's what that looks like.

35
00:01:11,550 --> 00:01:13,470
Start at one meter, and call the power

36
00:01:13,470 --> 00:01:14,910
there one hundred percent.

37
00:01:14,910 --> 00:01:17,070
At two meters, you're down to twenty five

38
00:01:17,070 --> 00:01:19,390
percent. At four meters, about six

39
00:01:19,470 --> 00:01:21,710
percent. The curve looks like it flattens

40
00:01:21,710 --> 00:01:23,950
out, but it never really slows down.

41
00:01:23,950 --> 00:01:25,870
Every doubling of distance takes away

42
00:01:25,870 --> 00:01:27,630
another three quarters of whatever was

43
00:01:27,630 --> 00:01:28,330
left.

44
00:01:28,510 --> 00:01:30,990
Engineers usually use decibels for this,

45
00:01:30,990 --> 00:01:33,230
because signals cover such a huge range.

46
00:01:33,230 --> 00:01:35,950
A change in decibels is ten times the log

47
00:01:36,030 --> 00:01:37,390
of the power ratio.

48
00:01:37,390 --> 00:01:39,550
A quarter of the power is about minus six

49
00:01:39,550 --> 00:01:42,510
decibels. One hundredth is minus twenty.

50
00:01:42,510 --> 00:01:45,070
So every doubling of distance costs about

51
00:01:45,070 --> 00:01:47,230
six decibels, and every ten times the

52
00:01:47,230 --> 00:01:49,070
distance costs twenty.

53
00:01:49,070 --> 00:01:52,430
Your phone measures Wi Fi in dBm, decibels

54
00:01:52,430 --> 00:01:54,030
compared to one milliwatt.

55
00:01:54,030 --> 00:01:56,510
Minus thirty dBm is a thousandth of a

56
00:01:56,510 --> 00:01:58,910
milliwatt. Minus seventy is one ten

57
00:01:59,070 --> 00:02:01,160
billionth of a watt, and a phone can often

58
00:02:01,160 --> 00:02:02,600
still browse on that.

59
00:02:02,600 --> 00:02:04,200
Here's a rough guide.

60
00:02:04,200 --> 00:02:07,080
Around minus thirty dBm is an extremely

61
00:02:07,080 --> 00:02:09,240
strong signal, right next to the router.

62
00:02:09,240 --> 00:02:10,920
Minus fifty is excellent.

63
00:02:10,920 --> 00:02:13,000
Around minus sixty seven is a common

64
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:15,240
design target for demanding uses like

65
00:02:15,240 --> 00:02:17,400
voice calls. Minus seventy can still

66
00:02:17,400 --> 00:02:19,000
support everyday internet use.

67
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:20,760
Around minus eighty, connections may

68
00:02:20,760 --> 00:02:21,880
become unreliable.

69
00:02:21,880 --> 00:02:24,040
And near minus ninety, staying connected

70
00:02:24,040 --> 00:02:25,000
can be difficult.

71
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:26,520
These aren't hard cutoffs.

72
00:02:26,520 --> 00:02:28,360
Interference and crowded networks matter

73
00:02:28,440 --> 00:02:29,240
too.

74
00:02:29,240 --> 00:02:31,880
Now let's test distance alone, in ideal

75
00:02:31,880 --> 00:02:34,360
open space. Say your phone reads minus

76
00:02:34,360 --> 00:02:35,800
thirty at one meter.

77
00:02:35,800 --> 00:02:37,560
Every time we double the distance, we lose

78
00:02:37,560 --> 00:02:39,000
another six decibels.

79
00:02:39,000 --> 00:02:41,000
Two meters, minus thirty six.

80
00:02:41,000 --> 00:02:42,030
Eight meters, minus forty eight.

81
00:02:42,040 --> 00:02:44,920
Thirty two meters, minus sixty.

82
00:02:44,920 --> 00:02:46,360
Because we're counting distance in

83
00:02:46,360 --> 00:02:48,440
doublings, which is a logarithmic scale,

84
00:02:48,440 --> 00:02:50,280
the points fall on a straight line.

85
00:02:50,280 --> 00:02:52,280
Even thirty two meters away, the model

86
00:02:52,440 --> 00:02:54,440
still gives a reasonably strong signal.

87
00:02:54,440 --> 00:02:56,360
With these numbers, distance alone doesn't

88
00:02:56,360 --> 00:02:57,800
explain a weak room.

89
00:02:58,070 --> 00:03:00,470
So what's happening in that one room?

90
00:03:00,470 --> 00:03:02,550
Usually, it's what the signal has to pass

91
00:03:02,550 --> 00:03:05,270
through. Every wall adds its own loss,

92
00:03:05,270 --> 00:03:06,630
measured in decibels.

93
00:03:06,630 --> 00:03:09,110
At two point four gigahertz, a drywall

94
00:03:09,110 --> 00:03:11,110
partition might cost a few decibels.

95
00:03:11,110 --> 00:03:12,790
Brick can cost several more.

96
00:03:12,790 --> 00:03:15,670
Thick or reinforced concrete can cost much

97
00:03:15,670 --> 00:03:16,630
more than that.

98
00:03:16,630 --> 00:03:19,030
And metal, like a steel door, can block

99
00:03:19,030 --> 00:03:20,950
the signal especially strongly.

100
00:03:20,950 --> 00:03:23,190
The exact amount depends on the material,

101
00:03:23,190 --> 00:03:25,670
the thickness and how the wall is built.

102
00:03:25,670 --> 00:03:27,990
Now let's build a simplified example.

103
00:03:27,990 --> 00:03:30,310
The bedroom is ten meters from the router.

104
00:03:30,310 --> 00:03:32,550
The signal crosses two drywall walls,

105
00:03:32,550 --> 00:03:34,710
which we'll call four decibels each, and

106
00:03:34,710 --> 00:03:36,660
one thick concrete wall, which we'll call

107
00:03:36,670 --> 00:03:39,110
twenty two. That's a tough wall, chosen

108
00:03:39,110 --> 00:03:40,310
for this example.

109
00:03:40,310 --> 00:03:41,670
Distance first.

110
00:03:41,670 --> 00:03:43,510
Ten times the distance costs twenty

111
00:03:43,510 --> 00:03:46,150
decibels, so minus thirty becomes minus

112
00:03:46,150 --> 00:03:49,191
fifty. Then the drywall: minus fifty four,

113
00:03:49,201 --> 00:03:50,619
minus fifty eight.

114
00:03:50,710 --> 00:03:53,030
Then the concrete: minus eighty.

115
00:03:53,190 --> 00:03:54,790
That's weak enough to make the connection

116
00:03:54,790 --> 00:03:57,670
unreliable. In a real house, reflections

117
00:03:57,670 --> 00:03:59,590
make the signal jump around, but adding

118
00:03:59,590 --> 00:04:02,070
the losses gives a useful first estimate.

119
00:04:02,100 --> 00:04:04,020
Here's the same path on a graph.

120
00:04:04,020 --> 00:04:06,580
The dashed curve is ideal open space.

121
00:04:06,580 --> 00:04:08,580
It only drops to minus fifty at ten

122
00:04:08,580 --> 00:04:11,380
meters. The solid line is our example.

123
00:04:11,380 --> 00:04:13,380
Every time it hits a wall, it steps

124
00:04:13,380 --> 00:04:14,340
straight down.

125
00:04:14,340 --> 00:04:17,140
Drywall at three meters, drywall at six,

126
00:04:17,140 --> 00:04:19,060
and thick concrete at eight.

127
00:04:19,060 --> 00:04:20,500
By the bedroom, our estimate

128
00:04:26,900 --> 00:04:29,620
is minus

129
00:04:39,780 --> 00:04:43,300
eighty, around where

130
00:04:43,540 --> 00:04:45,540
connections can get unreliable.

131
00:04:45,540 --> 00:04:47,700
Now move the router toward the middle of

132
00:04:47,700 --> 00:04:49,460
the home, so the signal skips the

133
00:04:49,460 --> 00:04:51,780
concrete. In our example, the bedroom is

134
00:04:51,780 --> 00:04:54,260
now five meters away, through one drywall

135
00:04:54,340 --> 00:04:56,980
wall. Five meters costs about fourteen

136
00:04:56,980 --> 00:04:59,300
decibels, so minus forty four.

137
00:04:59,300 --> 00:05:01,530
One wall takes four more: minus forty

138
00:05:01,540 --> 00:05:04,580
eight. That's thirty two decibels better

139
00:05:04,580 --> 00:05:07,150
than before, in our simplified model.

140
00:05:07,150 --> 00:05:09,710
Real results vary, but fewer obstacles can

141
00:05:09,710 --> 00:05:11,470
make a big difference.

142
00:05:11,470 --> 00:05:13,620
If you can't move the router, bring the Wi

143
00:05:13,630 --> 00:05:15,390
Fi closer to the room.

144
00:05:15,390 --> 00:05:17,390
A second access point wired back to the

145
00:05:17,390 --> 00:05:19,230
router works especially well.

146
00:05:19,230 --> 00:05:22,030
A mesh system can help too, but its units

147
00:05:22,030 --> 00:05:24,190
need a good link to each other, so don't

148
00:05:24,190 --> 00:05:26,190
put one where the signal is already almost

149
00:05:26,190 --> 00:05:29,470
gone. Keep the router high, in the open,

150
00:05:29,470 --> 00:05:31,470
away from big metal objects.

151
00:05:31,470 --> 00:05:33,790
And two point four gigahertz usually gets

152
00:05:33,790 --> 00:05:35,710
through walls better, while five and six

153
00:05:35,710 --> 00:05:37,950
gigahertz can be faster up close.

154
00:05:38,190 --> 00:05:40,350
The same idea shows up elsewhere.

155
00:05:40,350 --> 00:05:42,510
Light from a small lamp and sound from a

156
00:05:42,510 --> 00:05:44,670
speaker in an open field behave this way

157
00:05:44,670 --> 00:05:46,430
under ideal conditions.

158
00:05:46,430 --> 00:05:48,510
Double the distance, a quarter of the

159
00:05:48,510 --> 00:05:50,350
power per square meter, minus six

160
00:05:50,350 --> 00:05:52,910
decibels. Ten times the distance, one

161
00:05:52,990 --> 00:05:55,070
hundredth, minus twenty.

162
00:05:55,070 --> 00:05:58,270
So why does your Wi Fi die in one room?

163
00:05:58,270 --> 00:06:00,910
In ideal open space, power follows the

164
00:06:00,910 --> 00:06:03,460
inverse square law: double the distance, a

165
00:06:03,460 --> 00:06:06,260
quarter of the power, minus six decibels.

166
00:06:06,260 --> 00:06:09,060
But inside a home, walls can cost as much

167
00:06:09,060 --> 00:06:11,140
as distance, or much more.

168
00:06:11,140 --> 00:06:13,130
Count the walls, not just the meters.

169
00:06:13,140 --> 00:06:14,660
Want to test it?

170
00:06:14,660 --> 00:06:17,380
A Wi Fi analyzer app shows your signal in

171
00:06:17,380 --> 00:06:20,020
dBm. Check it near the router, then in the

172
00:06:20,020 --> 00:06:20,900
problem room.

173
00:06:20,900 --> 00:06:23,380
It measures strength, not speed, but it

174
00:06:23,380 --> 00:06:25,860
shows you where the decibels go.

175
00:06:25,860 --> 00:06:27,700
If you liked this video, please give it a

176
00:06:27,700 --> 00:06:29,700
like and subscribe for more everyday

177
00:06:29,700 --> 00:06:31,300
engineering. Thanks for watching!
